{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","statement":"The paper proposes Implicit ReasoNets (IRNs), composed of a global memory and a controller module, to learn multi-step relation paths in vector space and infer missing facts jointly without human-designed procedures.","source_span":"we propose Implicit ReasoNets (IRNs), that is composed of a global memory and a controller module to learn multi-step relation paths in vector space and infer missing facts jointly without any human-designed procedure."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","statement":"The proposed model achieves state-of-the-art results on popular knowledge base completion benchmarks without using any auxiliary information.","source_span":"Without using any axillary information, our proposed model achieves state-of-the-art results on popular knowledge base completion benchmarks."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","statement":"Previous works manually design relation paths of observed triplets in symbolic space (e.g. random walk) to learn multi-step relations during training.","source_span":"previous works manually design relation paths of observed triplets in symbolic space (e.g. random walk) to learn multi-step relations during training."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","statement":"The authors thank Scott Wen-Tau Yih, Kristina Toutanova, Jian Tang, Greg Yang, Adith Swaminathan, Xiaodong He, and Zachary Lipton for their feedback and discussions.","source_span":"We thank Scott Wen-Tau Yih, Kristina Toutanova, Jian Tang, Greg Yang, Adith Swaminathan, Xiaodong He, and Zachary Lipton for their thoughtful feedback and discussions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","statement":"The proposed method learns to traverse in vector space directly, without needing symbolic-space guidance.","source_span":"we propose learning to traverse in vector space directly without the need of symbolic space guidance."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","statement":"The paper includes an analysis section applying IRNs to a Shortest Path Synthesis Task.","source_span":"Inference Steps in KBC Analysis: Applying IRNs to a Shortest Path Synthesis Task"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What does IRN stand for and what two components make it up?","answer":"IRN stands for Implicit ReasoNets, and it is composed of a global memory and a controller module."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What limitation of prior knowledge base completion approaches motivated this paper's method?","answer":"Prior approaches manually designed relation paths of observed triplets in symbolic space (e.g. random walk), which are mostly uninformative and prohibitively expensive to explore exhaustively."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Does the proposed IRN model need auxiliary information to reach state-of-the-art performance on KB completion benchmarks?","answer":"No, it achieves state-of-the-art results on popular knowledge base completion benchmarks without using any auxiliary information."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Who did the authors acknowledge for feedback and discussions in this paper?","answer":"Scott Wen-Tau Yih, Kristina Toutanova, Jian Tang, Greg Yang, Adith Swaminathan, Xiaodong He, and Zachary Lipton."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What specific task is used to analyze the inference steps of IRNs in this paper?","answer":"A Shortest Path Synthesis Task."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0100","fact_refs":[4,2],"question":"How does IRN's approach to learning multi-step relations differ from prior symbolic-space methods like random walk?","answer":"IRN learns to traverse directly in vector space without needing symbolic-space guidance, whereas prior methods manually designed relation paths (e.g. via random walk) in symbolic space."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","statement":"The proposed multilingual NMT approach achieved improvements of up to 2.6 BLEU points in an under-resourced translation scenario.","source_span":"considerable improvements up to 2.6 BLEU points"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","statement":"The proposed approach uses only one encoder and one decoder to handle all languages involved, regardless of side.","source_span":"our approach allows us to integrate any language in any side of the encoder-decoder architecture with only one encoder and one decoder for all the languages involved."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","statement":"The attention mechanism (originally called the alignment model) was first introduced by BIBREF2 and integrated on the decoder side as feedforward layers.","source_span":"First introduced by BIBREF2 , attention mechanism is integrated in decoder side as feedforward layers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","statement":"This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.","source_span":"This work is licenced under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","statement":"A standard NMT encoder is a bidirectional recurrent encoder that encodes the source sentence into a fixed-length context vector.","source_span":"a bidirectional recurrent encoder reads every words INLINEFORM0 of a source sentence INLINEFORM1 and encodes a representation INLINEFORM2 of the sentence into a fixed-length vector"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","statement":"Recurrent units in NMT can be a simple RNN, an LSTM unit, or a GRU.","source_span":"Recurrent units in NMT can be a simple recurrent neural network unit (RNN), a Long Short-Term Memory unit (LSTM) BIBREF3 or a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) BIBREF4"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","statement":"The proposed approach requires no special network architecture treatment and learns a minimal number of free parameters using standard training.","source_span":"Our approach does not require any special treatment on the network architecture and it allows us to learn minimal number of free parameters in a standard way of training."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","statement":"The approach was also tested on a zero-resourced translation scenario where there is no direct parallel corpus between source and target languages.","source_span":"the approach has achieved interesting and promising results when applied in the translation task that there is no direct parallel corpus between source and target languages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How much of a BLEU score improvement did the proposed multilingual NMT approach achieve in the under-resourced translation scenario?","answer":"Up to 2.6 BLEU points."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How many encoders and decoders does the proposed unified multilingual NMT framework use to handle multiple languages?","answer":"Just one encoder and one decoder for all the languages involved, regardless of which side they appear on."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Who first introduced the attention mechanism described in this paper's NMT background, and where was it placed in the architecture?","answer":"BIBREF2 first introduced it (originally called the alignment model), integrated on the decoder side as feedforward layers."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Under what license is this paper published?","answer":"It is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What three types of recurrent units can be used in an NMT model according to this paper?","answer":"A simple RNN unit, an LSTM unit, or a GRU."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0101","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Besides under-resourced translation, what other demanding scenario was the multilingual NMT approach evaluated on?","answer":"A zero-resourced translation scenario, where there is no direct parallel corpus between the source and target languages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","statement":"TransE's score function is defined as f(h,r,t) = -||h + r - t||_2^2.","source_span":"f(h,r,t)) &= -\\Vert \\textbf {h}+\\mathbf {r}-\\mathbf {t}\\Vert _{2}^2"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","statement":"TransE requires entity embeddings to satisfy ||h||_2^2 = ||t||_2^2 = 1.","source_span":"satisfy $\\Vert \\textbf {h}\\Vert ^2_2=\\Vert \\mathbf {t}\\Vert ^2_2=1$"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","statement":"The paper introduces three neural models to encode text description information of entities, one of which is an attentive model that selects related information as needed.","source_span":"we introduce three neural models to encode the valuable information from text description of entity, among which an attentive model can select related information as needed"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","statement":"A gating mechanism is applied to integrate structural and textual representations into a unified architecture.","source_span":"a gating mechanism is applied to integrate representations of structure and text into a unified architecture."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","statement":"WordNet and Freebase are cited as examples of large-scale knowledge graphs.","source_span":"such as WordNet BIBREF0 , Freebase BIBREF1"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","statement":"A knowledge graph fact is represented as a triplet (Head Entity, Relation, Tail Entity).","source_span":"An instance of fact is represented as a triplet (Head Entity, Relation, Tail Entity)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","statement":"TransE's basic idea is that the relationship between two entities corresponds to a translation between their embeddings, i.e., h + r ≈ t.","source_span":"the relationship between two entities is supposed to correspond to a translation between the embeddings of entities, that is, $\\textbf {h}+ \\mathbf {r}\\approx \\mathbf {t}$"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","statement":"The source code of this paper will be made available on Github.","source_span":"Source codes of this paper will be available on Github."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is the formula for TransE's score function f(h,r,t)?","answer":"f(h,r,t) = -||h + r - t||²₂, i.e. the negative squared L2 norm of (h + r - t)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What normalization constraint does TransE impose on entity embeddings h and t?","answer":"TransE requires ||h||²₂ = ||t||²₂ = 1."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How many neural models does this paper introduce for encoding entity text descriptions, and what is special about one of them?","answer":"The paper introduces three neural models; one is an attentive model that can select related information from the text as needed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What mechanism does the paper use to combine an entity's structural and textual representations?","answer":"A gating mechanism that integrates the structure and text representations into a unified architecture."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What two large-scale knowledge graphs are mentioned as examples in this paper?","answer":"WordNet and Freebase."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How is a fact represented in a knowledge graph according to this paper?","answer":"As a triplet (Head Entity, Relation, Tail Entity)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0102","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Where does the paper say its source code will be made available?","answer":"On Github."}
